VMware vmxnet3 paravirtual Ethernet adapter, NDIS 5.1 miniport, PCI 15AD:07B0 (also emulated by QEMU:
-device vmxnet3)

The driver (vmxnet3.c, info.c) is new code, GPL-2.0-or-later.

Origin of the device interface
  Project:  OpenBSD
  Files:    sys/dev/pci/if_vmxreg.h revision 1.10 (2024/06/07) and sys/dev/pci/if_vmx.c revision 1.96
            (2026/06/23): the start-up order and the use of the rings only
  URL:      https://github.com/openbsd/src/blob/master/sys/dev/pci/if_vmxreg.h
  License:  ISC, notice kept in the header of vmxnet3.h
  Used:     the register offsets, the command numbers, the layouts of the transmit and receive
            descriptors and of the completion descriptors, of the driver shared structure and of the
            queue shared structures, the generation bit protocol of the rings, and the order of the start-up
            steps (revision selection, shared structure address, command enable). No code was copied:
            the OpenBSD driver is written for its own network stack and DMA API.

Not used: the Linux driver vmxnet3 (GPL-2.0-only with a vendor notice: not a licence this tree takes), and any
VMware binary driver.

What it does not do: offloads (checksum, TSO, LRO, VLAN tags, RSS), more than one queue, MSI and MSI-X (INTx
only), jumbo frames (MTU 1500), wake on LAN. The frames are copied between the packets and buffers that the
driver owns. Every value that the device writes (the completion indices, the frame lengths, the MAC address,
the link speed) is validated before it is used; the driver waits for nothing on the device.
